# std/STRUCTURE — How the Repo Is Organized

File: std/docs/STRUCTURE.oot

This file describes the layout of the openOODA standard library at
v1.0.0. Read it before you import anything.

## 1. Top-level layout

The repository has one tree. There is no monorepo sub-module. Every
file lives under the root, with a stable path freeze declared in
README.oot. After the 1.0.0 cut, no new top-level directories will
appear.

The root contains:

  - ANCHOR.oo        Front door for agents. Re-exports the 8 domain hubs.
  - README.oot       Top-level map. States the 6 laws and the domain tree.
  - VERSION          Version and compatibility contract (semver, plus
                     api_surface=8 as a structural check).
  - LICENSE          Dual MIT / Apache-2.0 license.
  - docs/            This directory. 4 .oot files plus the index.
  - examples/        Small programs that exercise one or two modules.
  - qa/              Invariants, KATs, adversarial vectors, caps tests.
  - audit/           Out-of-band audit state (not part of the API).
  - scripts/         Local helper scripts.
  - app/             Functional domain: applied software.
  - core/            Functional domain: universal primitives.
  - fs/              Functional domain: filesystem, OS, time.
  - hw/              Functional domain: hardware theory.
  - meta/            Functional domain: test and diagnostic infrastructure.
  - net/             Functional domain: network, transport, sync, actor.
  - science/         Functional domain: physics, chem, bio, econ, comm.
  - sec/             Functional domain: security.

The eight functional domains are the load-bearing structure. Each one
is a self-contained subtree. Every domain ships its own ANCHOR.oo
that re-exports its sub-hubs.

## 2. The 8 domains, and why exactly 8

The 8-domain split reflects functional alignment. One concept lives
in one place. No "misc", no "utils", no "common".

  - core/    Universal primitives. Every other domain depends on it.
  - fs/      Filesystem, OS surface, time, archive, compression.
  - net/     Network protocols, transport, sync, actor, telemetry.
  - sec/     Security primitives, laws, attestation, capability.
  - science/ Physics, chemistry, biology, economics, communications.
  - app/     AI, robotics, UI, data, agents, combat.
  - hw/      Hardware theory: cache, branch, memory, GPU, silicon.
  - meta/    Test and diagnostic infrastructure.

This is the Pillar 6 surface. The VERSION file declares
`api_surface=8`. The loader asserts that the std ANCHOR.oo exposes
exactly 8 public subdirectories. If a future change adds a 9th, the
loader fails closed. If a future change removes one, the loader
fails closed. The contract is structural, not behavioral.

## 3. ANCHOR.oo per directory

Every domain has an ANCHOR.oo at its root. Every sub-hub has its
own ANCHOR.oo. The pattern is the same at every level:

  1. Title (// # ...).
  2. Logline (// Logline: ...).
  3. Setup (// Setup: ...). One line. Lists required caps.
  4. Beats (// Beats: ...). A numbered list of the public surface.
  5. import statements that pull in the next level.
  6. Public functions re-exported as `<domain>_mod_*`.

External callers import the top-level ANCHOR.oo for the domain they
need. They do not import private siblings directly. Private siblings
follow a naming convention: `_law`, `_theory`, `_principle`,
`_invariants`, `_kat`, `_stubs`. These are the certification and
verification files. They are not part of the public API.

## 4. The 256-line cap

Every file in this repo is capped at 256 lines. The cap is enforced
by the loader, not by review. Two reasons:

  1. Reviewable surface. A 256-line file fits on one screen. An
     agent can read it, understand it, and verify it in one pass.
  2. Decomposition pressure. When a file grows past 256 lines, the
     right answer is to split, not to expand the cap. The cap is a
     forcing function for clean decomposition.

The cap applies to .oo source files, .oot doc files, README.oot, and
ANCHOR.oo alike. No file in the tree may exceed it.

## 5. What does not live in std/

  - The compiler (oodac). It is its own repo.
  - The package manager (opm). It is its own repo.
  - The CLI. It is its own repo.
  - The LSP and MCP servers. They are their own repos.
  - The website. It pulls from these docs but does not live here.

If you are looking for the toolchain, you are in the wrong repo.
If you are looking for a library, you are in the right place.
